Executive Summary:




The People’s Republic of China (PRC) is pushing its domestic computing ecosystem overseas despite U.S. export controls constraining access to high-end AI chips. Chinese firms have laid the groundwork for physical deployments of domestic hardware while pursuing “Token going global” (Token 出海), in which compute remains in the PRC while serving overseas clients. &nbsp;



China’s compute crunch is uneven. Shortages are concentrated in frontier hardware even as aggregate computing capacity expands. This enables Chinese firms to export compute without first achieving hardware parity with foreign competitors.&nbsp;



Overseas expansion itself may alleviate bottlenecks by creating demand and application scenarios that accelerate the iterative improvement of the PRC’s domestic AI ecosystem.








